    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d627">

                <pb facs="0866" n="852" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg852"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/7732</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by President Roosevelt to the Secretary of the Treasury</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Morgenthau</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-01-19">January 19, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I find a delayed letter from Madame Chiang<anchor n="46" xml:id="d627-f1"/> which
                    suggests that the Generalissimo would be willing to send Dr. Kung to Washington.
                    Don’t you think that would be a good idea and that we should accept it?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ranklin</hi>] D. R[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">oosevelt</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d627-f1" n="46" xml:id="d627-f1-content">
                <p>Dated December 5, 1943, <ref target="frus1943China#pg_178">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1943, China, p.
                    178</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
